<TopicPage slug="subjects/classics/foundational/homer-and-the-world-of-the-hero">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Classics begins for most students with Homer — and with good reason: the Iliad and Odyssey
    (composed in an oral tradition, written down around the 8th–7th centuries BCE) are the founding
    texts of Western literature and its first deep studies of anger, grief, homecoming and fame.
    "The world of the hero" is both a literary setting and a moral universe with its own currency:
    honour.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="two epics, one heroic universe" widget="equationStep" config={epicCfg} />

  <h2>The hero's terrible choice</h2>
  <p>
    Achilles is told a prophecy: a long life without glory, or a short one with fame forever. The
    Iliad is what that choice costs — his withdrawal costs the Greeks dear, the death of his friend
    Patroclus brings him back, and the poem ends not with victory but with grief: an old king
    begging for his son's body. Homer invented, in the first texts we have, the idea that a war
    story could be a tragedy of the victors.
  </p>

  <h2>How the poems were made</h2>
  <p>
    The epics' repeated phrases — "swift-footed Achilles", "rosy-fingered Dawn" — are not
    decoration but machinery: formulae that let a bard compose in strict metre in real time.
    Milman Parry and Albert Lord showed in the 20th century that living oral poets work exactly
    this way, settling the ancient "Homeric question": the poems are the crystallised memory of a
    centuries-long singing tradition.
  </p>

  <h2>Why read them now</h2>
  <p>
    Because the questions have not aged: what is worth dying for, what war does to those who fight
    it, what home means after twenty years away. The Odyssey keeps spawning retellings; the Iliad
    keeps being quoted about every war since. That afterlife — the classical tradition — is
    literally the second half of this subject.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OCR A Level Classical Civilisation H408 specification; Homer, trans. Lattimore/Fagles; Graziosi, <em>Inventing Homer</em>; Parry &amp; Lord's oral-formulaic research.</p>
</TopicPage>
